    {
      "id": "be-s6-linear-demand-tr-mr-q-f04387e8",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain, using a linear demand curve and the total revenue curve, how elasticity changes along the demand line.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** A straight-line demand curve does not have one elasticity: it changes along the line.\n\n**TR along the line.** With P = 80, 60, 40, 20 at Q = 10, 20, 30, 40, total revenue is 800, 1200, 1200 and 800. TR rises, peaks, and falls.\n\n**Elastic region.** At high prices and low quantities TR rises as Q rises: a price cut raises revenue, so demand is elastic.\n\n**Unit elastic point.** At the peak of TR, demand is unit elastic.\n\n**Inelastic region.** At low prices and high quantities TR falls as Q rises: a price cut lowers revenue, so demand is inelastic.\n\n**Conclusion.** Elasticity depends on where you are on the demand curve.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "TR values 800, 1200, 1200, 800",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#12"
        },
        {
          "point": "Upper part elastic",
