    {
      "id": "be-s6-linear-demand-tr-mr-q-11f15a16",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain the link between demand, marginal revenue and elasticity for a linear inverse demand function.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** Marginal revenue (MR) tells you whether a firm is in the elastic or the inelastic part of a linear demand curve.\n\n**Formula.** For linear inverse demand, **MR(Q) = a + 2bQ**, where b < 0.\n\n**Rules.**\n- MR > 0: demand is elastic (TR is rising).\n- MR = 0: demand is unit elastic (TR is at its peak).\n- MR < 0: demand is inelastic (TR is falling).\n\n**Graph.** The MR line starts at the same point on the price axis as demand but falls more steeply.\n\n**Conclusion.** Reading the sign of MR is a quick check of elasticity.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "MR(Q) = a + 2bQ with b < 0",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#16"
        },
        {
          "point": "MR > 0 elastic",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#16"
        },
        {
          "point": "MR = 0 unit elastic",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#16"
        },
        {
          "point": "MR < 0 inelastic",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#16"
        },
        {
          "point": "Link to TR rising, peak, falling",
          "source_ref": "be--session-6#15"
        }
      ],
      "marks": 5.0,
      "difficulty": 3,
      "style_tag": "explain",
      "source_refs": [
